## Why This Is Asked

Autocomplete-like prefix queries with dynamic updates are common in databases and search systems. A Trie with counts cleanly supports insertions, deletions, and fast prefix queries, even with duplicates.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node pass-through counts
- O(len(word)) insertion/erasure; O(len(prefix)) prefix queries
- Correct handling of duplicates via counts, not just terminal markers

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.cnt = 0;
    this.end = false;
  }
}

class WordTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }

  insertWord(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
      node.cnt++;
    }
    node.end = true;
  }

  eraseWord(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return;
      node = node.next.get(ch);
      node.cnt--;
    }
    node.end = false;
  }

  countWordsWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    return node.cnt;
  }
}
```

## How It Works

- **insertWord**: Traverses/creates nodes for each character, incrementing the pass-through count at each node
- **eraseWord**: Traverses existing nodes, decrementing counts; nodes with zero count can be pruned
- **countWordsWithPrefix**: Traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass-through count, representing all words that pass through this prefix

The pass-through count at each node represents how many words in the trie have that prefix, enabling O(len(prefix)) prefix queries while maintaining O(len(word)) updates.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical DS reasoning for streams where both max and total are needed efficiently, using a familiar two-stack queue pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Two-stack queue with per-element max
- Running sum maintenance for O(1) sum queries
- Amortized analysis of transfer between stacks

## Code Example
```javascript
class MaxQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.in = [];
    this.out = [];
    this.total = 0;
  }
  push(x) {
    const newMax = this.in.length === 0 ? x : Math.max(x, this.in[this.in.length - 1].max);
    this.in.push({ val: x, max: newMax });
    this.total += x;
  }
  pop() {
    if (this.out.length === 0) {
      while (this.in.length > 0) {
        const v = this.in.pop().val;
        const newMax = this.out.length === 0 ? v : Math.max(v, this.out[this.out.length - 1].max);
        this.out.push({ val: v, max: newMax });
      }
    }
    if (this.out.length === 0) return undefined;
    const popped = this.out.pop();
    this.total -= popped.val;
    return popped.val;
  }
  max() {
    const maxIn = this.in.length === 0 ? -Infinity : this.in[this.in.length - 1].max;
    const maxOut = this.out.length === 0 ? -Infinity : this.out[this.out.length - 1].max;
    if (maxIn === -Infinity && maxOut === -Infinity) return undefined;
    return Math.max(maxIn, maxOut);
  }
  sum() {
    return this.total;
  }
}
``` 

